man, reconstructed from /bin/man
================================

There is no source for man, so this is the disassembly, and the names in
man.ctl are what man.c is written against.  man.dist is a copy of the
9203-byte /bin/man off the 1.6 filesystem; man.dis is disas's output from
it, regenerated with "make man.dis".

nm -v gives the shape: text 8662 bytes at 0x0100, data 525 at 0x3000, no
bss and no symbol table.  With no bss the zeroed globals live in the data
segment, which is why nm -b reads mostly as zeros.  man's own text is
0x0116..0x0885 and everything above that is library.

It is Whitesmith's C, laid out exactly like /etc/init:

	                man       init
	c.switch      0x2256    0x3f62
	c.ent         0x227b    0x3f87
	c.ret         0x2282    0x3f8e
	c.ent2        0x2286    0x3f92
	c.ret2        0x229c    0x3fa8

and, as in init, the syscall stubs are in the DATA segment rather than the
text.  They are reached by the indirect trap form - "cf 00" followed by
the address of a stub, itself "cf <n>" and the argument words - so the
nine at 0x31c3..0x31ef are what named sbrk, creat, exec, gtty, open, read,
seek, stat and write.  close, exit, fork and wait trap directly and disas
labels those itself.


How the names were found
------------------------

Not from a symbol table; there isn't one.  The order was:

  - nm -v for the segment layout, nm -b for the data.  Two structures fall
    out of the data dump: a NULL-terminated table of 34 pointers into the
    text at 0x3177, which is perror's message list (and which is why the
    function right after those strings, 0x19a0, is perror), and the
    syscall stubs at 0x31c3.
  - calltree.py over the raw disassembly for the shape.  64 call targets;
    the two called most, 19 and 18 times, are c.ent and c.ent2, and the
    two jumped to most, 33 and 28, are their returns.
  - the string literals, which Whitesmith's puts in the TEXT segment, so
    they sit between the functions that use them and say what those
    functions are for.
  - and then reading the code.

The two man pages already in the tree - usr/man/man1/man.1 and help.1,
copied in beside this - turned out to confirm the search order after it
had been read out of the code rather than before.  man.1: "it omits
searching /usr/help, and searches /usr/man/man0, ..., /usr/man/man9 in
order".  help.1: "looks first in /usr/help ... Then, like man, it searches
/usr/man/man0, ..., /usr/man/man9".  That is what show() does.


Two things in the binary that are wrong
---------------------------------------

Reproduced in man.c, both of them, because they are what it does.

  1. The /usr/bin fallback in run() cannot work.  The child tries /bin/
     and then /usr/bin, and the strings are

	0x04e2  "/usr/bin"
	0x04eb  "/bin/"

     - the second has a trailing slash and the first does not, so the
     path built for the fallback is "/usr/binform".  /bin/form exists, so
     the first exec always succeeds and this has never been reached.

  2. show() complains about an empty name, or one starting with '.', and
     then carries on looking for it anyway - the object calls nodoc and
     falls through instead of returning.  The search then fails and says
     so a second time.

And one that is only odd: the exit status is 1 when man has shown
something and 0 from the usage, which is the wrong way round.  Nothing in
the system looks at it.


What the pages do not say
-------------------------

  - "-" as an argument.  It clears the flag that otherwise passes -t to
    form, and the flag is set by asking descriptor 1 for its modes, so
    normally -t goes when the output is a terminal and does not when it
    is a pipe.  Neither page mentions the argument.
  - The lettered subsections.  man.ctl's issection() takes a run of
    digits and nothing else, so "man 3s printf" reads 3s as a page name.
    The printed manual has 3s, 3m and 3c pages; this program cannot ask
    for them by section.


Library routines
----------------

man calls three that this tree's libc does not carry - equal 0x1647,
concat 0x1692, putstr 0x16f7 - and they are the same three /etc/init
needed.  Both files now have a copy, which is one too many; libc is where
they belong, and the addresses in each file say what to check a libc
version against.

The rest it uses are ordinary: fopen 0x0886, fclose 0x0b4f, fread 0x0bee,
exit 0x155e, perror 0x19a0, execv 0x1b54.


Not run yet
-----------

man.c compiles into the cross build.  It has not been run: the thing to
try it on is a Micronix boot with form(1) present, comparing its output
against the original binary's for the same page.
